WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.439 --> 00:00:04.070 align:start position:0%
 
to those  destroying these huts in the himalayan  forests  they're unwanted pieces of wood and  cement  but to the bakarwal and gujjar the  muslim nomadic tribes who lived here  their home at least for six months a  year  as they move between the forests and  mountainous pastures with their herds of  goats and sheep  we are yet to recover from the shark we  have been living here for generations  we are now worried for our livestock and  our children  i tried to plead with the demolition  team but they threatened us with a rest  and a fine some in the tribe received a  notice from the local forest department  saying their homes were illegal a few  days later  people arrived to tear them down  the himalayan region of kashmir is  controlled by india and pakistan in part  but  claimed by both in full last year  india revoked the region's special  status imposing restrictions on movement  and enforcing a communications blackout  critics say the government wants to  change the demographic of the muslim  majority region  and is carrying out the demolitions to  do that  they want to dislodge people gradually  from this place  throw them out make them homeless in  their own territory  so settle non-kashmiris  indian is there and that will facilitate  their agent off completely hinduising  kashmir  but local officials say they're only  following court orders  and that more than 60 000 people are  illegally squatting on the land  in india traditional forest dwellers are  usually protected by law  and tribal leaders and kashmir say that  should extend to  them  last year when the special status was  repealed the government published an  advertisement  that they were going to implement the  forest act law  in the region but the reality on the  ground is different  the government is working against us and  people  are suffering under pressure from the  government to move from the forest  and from encroaching cities some say  their traditional way of life  is becoming too difficult  we live in fear that we may face the  same fate as the others  we keep on thinking day and night that  our homes may be demolished  where would we go for generations  they've led a nomadic lifestyle  but that could soon become a thing of  the past  alex o'brien al jazeera

